  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py`, and all 12 tests pass, including 6 new ones.\n\n- **How it works:** it uses `divmod(cents, parts)` to get an equal share for each part plus a remainder. The first `remainder` parts each get one extra cent, so `split(1000, 3)` gives `[334, 333, 333]`. If `parts` is below 1 it raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests:** the new tests are in `tests/test_money.py`. They cover:\n  - an even split\n  - remainders, including more parts than cents (`split(2, 4)` gives `[1, 1, 0, 0]`)\n  - a single part\n  - a check that the parts always add back up to `cents`, over many inputs\n  - the `ValueError` for 0 and negative `parts`\n\n**Decision for you:** with negative amounts, the extra cent goes the \"wrong\" way. `split(-7, 3)` gives `[-2, -2, -3]`, so the largest magnitude ends up last, not first. The total is still correct. Your spec didn't cover negatives, so I left it alone. If refunds or credits will go through this, I can mirror the positive behaviour so it gives `[-3, -2, -2]`.",
